diff -ruN control.original/PIPELINES.md control/PIPELINES.md
--- control.original/PIPELINES.md	1970-01-01 00:00:00.000000000 +0000
+++ control/PIPELINES.md	2026-10-15 17:44:13.718042058 +0000
@@ -0,0 +1,49 @@
+# Pipeline execution and host boundary
+
+`cmd` streams pipelines of two or more builtins when none has redirections.
+Each stage has an independent run context and goroutine. Adjacent stages use
+`io.Pipe`: writes wait for reads, so intermediate storage is bounded rather
+than proportional to output. Closing a consumer closes its pipe reader and
+unblocks writers with `io.ErrClosedPipe`. Producers close writers to deliver
+EOF. All stages are joined, including after cancellation. Job kill/deadline
+checks run every 5 ms and close the internal endpoints. Steps retain command
+order, and the last stage determines pipeline status (no pipefail).
+
+`cat` now copies inputs incrementally, including on the spool path. `cmp`
+and `diff` still materialize inputs because their existing algorithms need
+them. Builtin `echo` constructs its argument output in memory. The response's
+existing inline output cap is unchanged; final job output remains on disk.
+
+Pipelines with wasm commands or redirections retain sequential disk spooling.
+Redirections deliberately retain the old ordering of filesystem side effects
+and descriptor bindings. Mixed pipelines are not split into streaming islands.
+Spool files are removed as each consumer finishes or its producer fails. This
+is not a claim that `yes.wasm | head.wasm` now terminates: it still needs a
+request timeout. Disk spooling does not bound disk usage for an endless producer.
+
+## Why host executions cannot stream yet
+
+`ExecSpec` in main.go exposes Stdin, Stdout, Stderr as path strings. `step` in
+exec.go starts one execution, polls until done, and then moves/appends its
+output. The imports in host_wasip1.go expose only start/status/kill/release/ready.
+There is no pipe allocation, guest stream read/write, descriptor transfer, or
+way to attach an execution to another execution's stdio. A regular file's
+current EOF is not a blocking pipe EOF. Polling/tailing it cannot fix a wasm
+consumer that has already observed EOF. WASI preview 1 also supplies no usable
+FIFO creation interface here. This change adds no speculative host imports.
+
+A host extension should allocate bounded anonymous pipes and return separate
+read/write endpoint IDs. ExecSpec must accept these endpoints for stdin/stdout/
+stderr, including stderr aliases. Start must retain child endpoint references;
+the control plane must be able to close its own references explicitly. Reads
+must block until data or final writer closure; writes must apply backpressure
+and report broken pipe when the final reader closes. A nonblocking host read/
+write API with pollable readiness is needed to connect builtin stages to those
+same endpoints. Endpoint cleanup must cover failed starts, traps, cancellation,
+and release. The host must support these semantics for both wasip1 and wasip2.
+
+The future scheduler must resolve/apply redirections, start all stages before
+waiting, close unused endpoints, track and kill *all* active handles (job.handle
+currently tracks only one), join all executions, preserve per-stage steps in
+command order, and stop siblings on abnormal failure. Starting multiple current
+ExecSpecs against the same regular file does not provide these semantics.
